<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!-- SpotBugs project decisions: a rule, or a rule in part of the code, that the team has decided not
     to enforce. One Match per decision, with a comment giving the reason, for example:

       <!- - JPA entities share associations by design (reason ...) - ->
       <Match><Package name="~team\.projectpulse\..*"/><Bug pattern="EI_EXPOSE_REP"/></Match>

     Unlike the baseline, this file may grow, but every entry is a reviewed decision. -->
<FindBugsFilter>
  <!-- D1 (2026-11-13): EI_EXPOSE_REP and EI_EXPOSE_REP2 are off project-wide. The rule guards an
       object's internal state against callers it does not trust. In Project Pulse every
       finding (219 on 2026-11-01) is one of three kinds of sharing the design depends on:
       - Spring beans (25 classes): a constructor stores an injected collaborator, such as
         SectionController.sectionService. Sharing that singleton is what dependency injection is for.
       - JPA entities (23 classes): getters and setters on Hibernate-managed associations and
         collections. Services change associations through the getter, for example
         TeamService's from.getTeams().remove(team). A defensive copy would make that a silent
         no-op, and replacing a managed collection in a setter breaks dirty checking and orphan removal.
       - Records and response DTOs (14 classes) and request-scoped report objects: built per
         request and serialized by Jackson straight away; no other code holds a reference to them.
       Nothing outside the application runs in the JVM, so nothing untrusted can exploit the
       shared references. -->
  <Match>
    <Or>
      <Bug pattern="EI_EXPOSE_REP"/>
      <Bug pattern="EI_EXPOSE_REP2"/>
    </Or>
  </Match>
</FindBugsFilter>
